# SYNOPSIS

```usage
man [-tw] [section ...] title [node]
lookman key ...
sig name ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`man` prints the page <title> of the manual, from the first <section> given that has one, or from the first of all eight.
A <title> is a page's name, or any name a page documents, which the index finds: `man lookman` prints this page.
With a <node>, it prints that node of the page alone; `man -t` lists a page's nodes.
The page is refilled to 80 columns, its headings at the margin and its text indented, as Plan 9's are; links are printed as written, `rc(1)`, so they can be plumbed.
There is no pager.

: `-t`
  Print the page's contents instead: each heading, each subheading indented, and each node as `@` and its id, then its title.
: `-w`
  Print the page's path instead.

`lookman` prints the pages about every <key>: each name in the index whose name, summary, node title or keys holds every <key>, ignoring case.
Each page, or node, is printed once, as the `man` command that shows it, then `#` and what it is about.

`sig` prints the C declarations of the functions <name> from the SYNOPSIS of their section 2 pages, each on one line, for pasting into code.
A <name> is a name the page documents, such as `futex_wait`, or its C function's, such as `vx_futex_wait`.

The manual is described by guide(6), its sections by `intro` in each section, such as intro(1).

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% man -t guide
SYNOPSIS
DESCRIPTION
@header The header
@blocks Blocks
@fences Fences
@inline Text inside a line
@nodes Nodes
EXAMPLES
FILES
SEE ALSO
DIAGNOSTICS
BUGS
% lookman format
man 6 guide # the manual's page format
man 6 intro # introduction to file formats
% sig futex_wake
int64_t vx_futex_wake(const _Atomic uint32_t *word, uint32_t count);
```

# FILES

: `/lib/man/<sect>/<page>`
  The pages.
: `/lib/man/index/`
  The index: one ndb file for each tree of pages, the base system's `base`.

# SEE ALSO

guide(6), intro(1)

# DIAGNOSTICS

`man` says `man: no page for` <title> when no section has it, and that is its exit string.
`lookman` exits `not found` when no page matches, and `sig` `not found` when a <name> has no declaration.

# BUGS

`man` refills to 80 columns whatever the terminal's width, since nothing yet tells it the width.
`lookman` matches each <key> anywhere in a word, not only whole words.
